    "body": "@annevk Can you provide an exact meaning of \"When\", as applied to the current issue? Do you mean in relation to adjacent `<link>` elements? At first glance this paragraph\r\n\r\n> Unless otherwise specified for a given rel keyword, the element must delay the load event of the element's node document until all the attempts to obtain the resource and its critical subresources are complete.\r\n\r\nappears to provide a means to essentially prevent the `<body>` or even adjacent `<link>` elements from being reached by possibly requesting a resource which is, for example\r\n\r\n1) is substantial relevant to `Content-Length` and the client resources; e.g., RAM\r\n\r\n2) potentially causing some form of internal \"timeout\", if the duration of the request  is observed at browser source code\r\n\r\nthat is, the request could be blocking as to the entire `document`? \r\n\r\nIf the element is capable of blocking the `load` event of `document` from being dispatched it from perspective here it would be beneficial to users to be able to get `progress` events relevant to the request outside of the `document` `load` event, specif as to the expected level of blocking for the `rel` type of the element at each \"spin\" of  [spin the event loop](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/webappapis.html#spin-the-event-loop), perhaps fed to a `ReadableStream`?\r\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
